.TH "LMM" "1" "Nov 2026" "lmm 2.3.0" "User Commands"

.SH NAME
lmm-update - Check for or apply mod updates


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBlmm update [mod-id] [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Check for available updates or update specific mods.

.PP
Without arguments, checks all installed mods for updates and prints a
table (auto-update-policy mods are then applied automatically; pass --all
to apply every available update). With a mod ID, checks and updates just
that mod. If the same mod ID is installed from more than one source in
the profile, use -s/--source to disambiguate (the error names the sources
in conflict). -s/--source is resolved once up front either way, so on a
game with more than one configured source it also avoids an interactive
source prompt for the bulk check.

.PP
--from-file <archive> updates the mod from an archive you downloaded by
hand - for a source that refuses API downloads (e.g. a CurseForge mod
whose author disabled third-party downloads). The source, mod ID, game
and profile come from the installed mod. When the update check advertises
a file, the archive's name is compared with it (case-insensitively,
ignoring a browser's " (1)" duplicate suffix): on a match its version and
file ID are recorded, so later update checks stay correct; another file -
a different flavor or loader's build - is refused unless you pass
--accept-mismatch. Without an advertised file, a file the source lists
under the archive's name supplies them, else the version comes from the
archive's name or --version. The lock and update policy are kept, the
previous version is preserved for 'lmm update rollback', and hooks run as
for any update.

.PP
A mod the profile's collection locks (a collection import locks each mod
it pins) is not checked at all: the collection decides its version, so the
check prints one line counting those mods instead of a row apiece, and
\&'lmm profile sync <profile> --collection' updates them. The collection
itself is checked instead: for a NexusMods collection, one request asks
for its latest revision, and a line says whether a newer one than the
installed revision is out. A collection imported from an https address or
a file can't be checked that cheaply; the line says so. Unlocking a mod
('lmm mod unlock') makes it yours and returns it to the check. A lock you
set yourself is still checked, and its update shown as locked.

.PP
--skip-all skips every update the check offers now - 'lmm mod
skip-update' for each, so nothing is pinned and a newer version is offered
again - and --unskip-all clears every skipped update. Neither takes a mod
ID, --all, --dry-run or --from-file.

.PP
If the update check itself fails partway through (e.g. a source outage),
whatever was learned before the failure is still printed and the command
exits non-zero rather than silently claiming success.

.PP
For a compile-deploy game (e.g. Icarus), a compiled mod whose game base pak
has changed since it was last compiled ("recompile needed") is checked and
applied through this same command: no new version, just a same-version
recompile against the current base pak.

.PP
--json prints exactly one JSON document to stdout, in one of two shapes:
  - Bulk check (no mod ID): {game_id, profile, updates: [...], skipped:
    {pinned, local, locked?, collection?, collection_sync?, updates?},
    error?}. skipped.locked counts the mods the collection locks (never
    checked), skipped.collection names it, and collection_sync says it
    has an origin 'lmm profile sync --collection' updates from.
    collection_revision? is the collection checked as a whole: {name,
    origin, source_id?, installed, latest?, available?, checked, reason?,
    error?, sync?} - origin "source", "url" or "file"; reason "url",
    "file" or "unsupported" when it was not asked. error is
    present when the check itself failed partway through; updates/skipped
    still reflect whatever was learned first. Each updates[] entry carries the whole installed mod
    under "installed_mod" plus "new_version"; "locked": true means the
    update is reported but will not be applied until the lock moves or
    clears, and "recompile_needed": true with a "recompile_reason" marks
    a base-pak staleness row (new_version equals the installed version -
    the mod itself hasn't changed).
  - Single mod (a mod ID given): {mod, name, from_version, to_version,
    changelog, status, reason, warnings, notes}, where "mod" is the
    profile reference {source_id, mod_id, version, locked}. status is one
    of "updated", "up_to_date", "skipped", "available" (--dry-run),
    "recompiled", or "recompile_available" (--dry-run, same-version
    base-pak recompile); reason is set only when status is "skipped"
    ("pinned", "local", or "locked").
  - With --from-file and --dry-run, the update-from-file plan: {archive,
    archive_name, mod, from_version, to_version, file_ids, match,
    matched_file?, match_normalized?, advertised?, locked, refusal?,
    files, hooks, warnings}. match is "advertised", "mismatch", "listed"
    or "none". Without --dry-run, the single-mod document above.
  - With --skip-all or --unskip-all: {game_id, profile, mods: [{source_id,
    mod_id, name, version}], applied, not_applied}, the mods whose skip was
    set (version: the one skipped) or cleared (version: the one the skip
    held); applied counts them.
  - 'update rollback' emits the rollback document: {mod, mod_name,
    from_version, to_version, status, reason, warnings, notes}, with
    status "rolled_back" or "skipped".

.PP
Examples:
  lmm update --game skyrim-se                    # Check all mods for updates
  lmm update 12345 --game skyrim-se              # Update specific mod
  lmm update 12345 --game skyrim-se --source nexusmods  # Disambiguate by source
  lmm update --game skyrim-se --all              # Apply all available updates
  lmm update --game skyrim-se --dry-run          # Show what would update
  lmm update --game skyrim-se --skip-all         # Skip every offered update
  lmm update --game skyrim-se --unskip-all       # Offer every skipped update again
  lmm update 12345 --game wow --from-file ~/Downloads/Mod-2.0.zip  # Update from a downloaded archive


.SH OPTIONS
\fB--accept-mismatch\fP[=false]
	with --from-file: update even when the archive is not the file the update check advertised

.PP
\fB--all\fP[=false]
	apply all available updates

.PP
\fB--dry-run\fP[=false]
	show what would update without applying

.PP
\fB--fomod-choices\fP=""
	answer FOMOD installer questions from a JSON file (the profile's fomod: shape or Vortex's choices array; key it by source:id when several mods ask, or by source:id#file-id for one file's installer)

.PP
\fB--fomod-defaults\fP[=false]
	answer FOMOD installer questions with the installer's own preselection

.PP
\fB-f\fP, \fB--force\fP[=false]
	continue even if hooks fail

.PP
\fB--from-file\fP=""
	update the mod from an archive you downloaded (for a source that refuses API downloads)

.PP
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for update

.PP
\fB-p\fP, \fB--profile\fP=""
	profile to check (default: active profile)

.PP
\fB--refresh\fP[=false]
	ask every mod's source again: bypass cached source metadata, locally cached source indexes (Steam Workshop caches for hours; the Thunderstore index for six) and the record of mods unchanged since the last check

.PP
\fB--skip-all\fP[=false]
	skip every update the check offers now, without pinning (each mod's offer is hidden until a newer version appears)

.PP
\fB-s\fP, \fB--source\fP=""
	mod source for a single-mod update (default: the sole configured source; prompts when several are configured). A bulk check never needs it

.PP
\fB--unskip-all\fP[=false]
	clear every skipped update, so the check offers each again

.PP
\fB--version\fP=""
	with --from-file: the version to record (default: the matched source file's, else the archive name's)


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--config\fP=""
	config directory (default: $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/lmm or ~/.config/lmm)

.PP
\fB--data\fP=""
	data directory (default: $XDG_DATA_HOME/lmm or ~/.local/share/lmm)

.PP
\fB-g\fP, \fB--game\fP=""
	game ID to operate on

.PP
\fB--json\fP[=false]
	output JSON instead of text; mutating commands print their result, --dry-run prints the plan; never prompts

.PP
\fB--log-level\fP="off"
	diagnostic log level written to stderr (off, error, warn, info, debug)

.PP
\fB--no-color\fP[=false]
	disable colored output (NO_COLOR env is also honored)

.PP
\fB--no-hooks\fP[=false]
	disable all hooks

.PP
\fB-v\fP, \fB--verbose\fP[=false]
	verbose output


